@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmw:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/works/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the forty-two gods of the hall of the two Maati, named
#
# traditions/egyptian-ritual.ttl entered the apparatus of chapter 125 as a
# single concept, pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina, on the ground that the failure
# condition of the whole corpus is stated there: the doorway refuses passage
# to a man who cannot tell its name. That node said what the chapter does. It
# did not carry a single one of the names, and a tradition whose operation
# consists in knowing names is not entered until the names are in the graph.
# pm:Naming exists for exactly this and the reason is at ontology/naming.ttl:
# a name is a harder identity than a concept, because the three grounds SPEC
# §14 gives for agreement without contact do not produce the same sequence of
# sounds.
#
# The forty-two are the first instalment. The doorkeepers of the arits of
# chapter 144, the pylon gods of chapters 145 and 146, the gods of the
# fourteen aats of chapters 149 and 150 and the named gods of the chapters
# already read are not here and are not claimed.
#
# Where the identifiers come from, because this is the decision the file
# stands or falls on. AGENTS.md requires the name form of the leading
# transliteration of the edition used, not of a translation, and two
# independent runs must arrive at the same identifier. The Papyrus of Ani of
# 1895 is the only public-domain edition available to this run that prints a
# transliteration at all: Budge sets it interlinearly under the hieroglyphs
# with his English beneath. That is the leading transliteration here and the
# forms below are taken from it, address by address, forty-two of them in the
# order of plates XXXI and XXXII.
#
# The text used is a scanned one and its transliteration line is the worst-set
# line on the page — diacritics, the Greek chi Budge uses for kh, and his
# apostrophe for tch are the first things a scanner loses. Every form was
# therefore repaired by hand against Budge's own Hieroglyphic Vocabulary to
# the Theban Recension of 1911, which marks the relevant entries with the
# words the name of one of the Forty-two Judges in the Hall of Osiris and
# whose headword line is set in ordinary roman. Thirty-nine of the forty-two
# are confirmed there under a headword; the source field of each node names
# both volumes, and skos:altLabel carries the other form wherever the two
# differ. Three are carried by the 1895 line alone, because the vocabulary has
# no marked entry for them: addresses 2, 17 and 22.
#
# What this does not claim. These are Budge's forms of the 1890s and 1900s and
# they are not the transliteration of current Egyptology; Usekh-nemmet is
# wsḫ-nmtt and Her-f-ha-f is ḥr.f-ḥꜣ.f in any modern handbook. The
# identifiers are reproducible from a named public-domain edition, which is
# what the rule asks for, and they are not correct Egyptian. A run with access
# to a modern critical edition should treat every identifier here as a form to
# be superseded and should say so at pmw:BookOfTheDead rather than renaming in
# silence.
#
# What the vocabulary showed that the chapter does not. Budge's index to the
# same recension marks judge names that do not occur in the Ani sequence at
# all — Uatch-nesert, Uarekht, Utent, Maa-ha-f, Neb-nebu, Then-ret, Serekhi,
# Ra. The number forty-two is therefore a count of slots and not of names, and
# the finding is entered as a search at examinations/searches-egyptian.ttl
# rather than as prose here.
#

pmc:AegyptiaConfessioNegativa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    rdfs:label "The forty-two addresses: one god, one denial"@en, "Die zweiundvierzig Anreden: ein Gott, eine Verneinung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The form the second half of chapter 125 takes in the Papyrus of Ani. Forty-two times the dead man says Hail, followed by a name, followed by the place the god comes forth from, followed by one thing he has not done. Nothing else stands in the unit: no petition, no offering, no act. The denial is bound to the god by position alone, and the binding is the whole content of the address."@en ;
    skos:note "Two counts that do not agree, and they are in the text rather than read into it. There are forty-two addresses and there are forty-four denials, because address 33 carries I have not harmed and I have not done evil, and address 41 carries I have not carried off the food of the infant and I have not sinned against the god of my town. The apparatus is built to a number and the material does not divide by it."@en ;
    skos:note "Two denials are also repeated word for word: addresses 20 and 21 both say I have not defiled the wife of a man, and addresses 17 and 31 both say I have not been an eavesdropper. A list assembled to fill forty-two places behaves like one."@en ;
    skos:note "What the form is for is stated at pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina and not here: the man passes because he can say the name. The denial is what he says once he has the name right, and no part of the chapter makes the passage depend on the denial being true."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, the address to the forty-two gods, Papyrus of Ani, in the transliteration and English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: British Museum, 1895, plates XXXI-XXXII, pp. 198-203" .

#
# The forty-two, in the order of the plates
#

pmc:AegyptiaUsekhNemmet a pm:Naming ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    rdfs:label "Usekh-nemmet"@en, "Usekh-nemmet"@de ;
